    "https://jobs.adelaide.edu.au/jobs/the-elizabeth-wood-research-fellowship-in-musicology-adelaide-cbd-sa-australia": {
      "title": "The Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship in Musicology",
      "description": "The Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship in Musicology - Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia Manage your settings Search Jobs Visit adelaideuni.edu.au Kiosk mode Together, we will define Adelaide University Together, we will define Adelaide University Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Jobs near me The Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship in Musicology Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 Casual Campaign Casual Add to favourites View favourites Adelaide University invites applications for musicologists for the Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship, whose work embodies this spirit of critical and disciplinary transformation. Two full-year fellowships are available. At Adelaide University, we create the opportunities you need to achieve your ambitions – because when you thrive, we thrive. We are transforming education for contemporary learners and global citizens. Building on a proud legacy and shaped by bold ambition, it’s a place of excellence and equity, where our vibrant community of staff are united by our purpose to inspire Australia’s future change-makers and create a better tomorrow. Work that matters The Elder Conservatorium of Music and School of Performing Arts unite rich tradition and innovative performance; we empower creative thinkers in music and the performing arts to transform their passion into professional success. Learn alongside celebrated artists, collaborate within a vibrant community, and access career pathways across music, acting, dance, stage management, production, and arts leadership. Your journey starts here. About the Fellowship Adelaide University invites applications for the Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship in Musicology. The Fellowship, generously funded by Professor Sally Macarthur, is named in honour of the world-renowned, Australian-born musicologist Dr Elizabeth Wood. Dr Wood's pioneering work began at Adelaide University, where she wrote the first history of Australian opera. Following her move to the United States in 1977, she undertook groundbreaking research in feminist, and gay and lesbian musicology, and produced a series of highly original critical studies of the English composer Ethel Smyth. Her landmark essay \"Sapphonics\" and her co-edited volume Queering the Pitch: The New Gay and Lesbian Musicology (1994) helped establish an entirely new field of scholarship. Dr Wood profoundly changed how we understand music as a cultural phenomenon, revealing how music, as a dynamic, ever-changing art form, is inseparably bound up with questions of gender, sexuality and identity, and transforming how generations of scholars have thought about music ever since. The Fellowship aims to build on the spirit of Wood’s trailblazing legacy. For the purposes of the Fellowship, musicology is defined as the academic and scholarly study of music, encompassing music’s history, theory, aesthetics, cultural contexts, and social meanings.  Unlike research driven from the practice of music itself—such as performance or composition—musicology generates knowledge through methods drawn not only from music practice but also from the humanities, creative arts, social sciences, and, increasingly, the sciences. It is an interdisciplinary inquiry into music as a site where sound, body, technology, power, identity, and affect intersect and, more recently, where agency is understood as distributed across human and non -human phenomena. It draws on close analysis of sonic and notational texts, pays ethnographic attention to musical communities, undertakes archival and historiographical work, and utilises theoretical frameworks from critical theory and philosophy—including non-representational paradigms such as new materialism and agential realism—as well as disability studies, postcolonial thought, and media studies. The field has moved decisively away from positivist or formalist orientations towards an understanding of music not as a self-contained aesthetic object but as a relational practice, always embedded in bodies, technologies, institutions, and histories of inequality. Through recent developments it has moved beyond simply describing music's structures or charting its histories to interrogating the very conditions under which musical knowledge is produced, drawing richly from the most destabilising currents in contemporary thought. With this orientation as its foundation—and springing from the pioneering work of Elizabeth Wood, who invented highly imaginative methods, developing her own conceptual tools drawn from other disciplines—Adelaide University seeks to attract musicologists for this Fellowship whose work embodies this spirit of critical and disciplinary transformation. Applicants should identify, first and foremost, as musicologists or ethnomusicologists, and their work should demonstrate familiarity with the disciplinary transformations outlined above, while showing clearly how their proposed project will advance the field through innovative, interdisciplinary methods. Experience There shall be two full-year fellowships awarded by Adelaide University (the University) to be known as “ as “Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship in Musicology” donated by Professor Sally Macarthur. The Fellowships have a fixed-term annual stipend and can be held for up to one year. The annual value of each stipend is $40,000 plus $10,000 top-up for travel expenses. The Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship in Musicology shall be open to: university graduates with a doctorate; or university graduates in the final year of their doctorate (who provide evidence of intended submission date within that year) Preference will be given to proposals that clearly articulate an alignment with current trends in musicology as outlined above The Elizabeth Wood Research Fellowship in Musicology is for the conduct of research in musicology and/or ethnomusicology The Fellowship cannot be held concurrently with a comparable fellowship. The number of Fellowships awarded in any given year will be subject to the discretion of the Selection Panel and according to available funding. Our commitment to inclusion and diversity We are committed to fostering a culture of inclusion where diversity is celebrated and everyone feels respected and valued. Adelaide University is an equal opportunity employer, committed to creating a safe, inclusive, and equitable workplace where everyone can thrive. We strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people with disability, and people of all ages, genders, cultural backgrounds, sexual orientations, and gender identities. We are committed to supporting flexible working arrangements and providing reasonable adjustments throughout the recruitment process. Launch your future with Adelaide University now Applicants should identify, first and foremost, as musicologists or ethnomusicologists, and their work should demonstrate familiarity with the disciplinary transformations outlined above while showing clearly how their proposed project will advance the field through innovative, interdisciplinary methods. Simply click on the Apply Now button and upload your CV and as part of your application (2000 words), submit the following: Title, Aims and background for the proposed research (1000 words) Research track record of applicant (500 words) The significance of the proposed research (200 words) Projected outputs (200 words) Proposed start and end time of the fellowship, including submission of the final report (100 words). Submit your application by 11:30pm Monday 19 October 2026 For further information about this opportunity, please contact Stephen Whittington (quoting reference number 493516 ): Stephen Whittington Director – Musicology Hub +61 (08) 8313 3689 | stephen.whittington@adelaide.edu.au Ready to start your application? Apply here. First Name (required) eb2e4f8c Last Name (required) 8a368474 Email (required) f6ee5376 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Ready to start your application? Apply here. First Name (required) a8160820 Last Name (required) d0ad14a8 Email (required) 0e86a771 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Geoff Williams Fellowship in Physics N/A, SA, Australia School of Physics, Chemistry and Earth Sciences Build your academic career through physics research, teaching, mentoring and collaboration across leading research programs. Exhibitions Coordinator Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 Community and Regional Engagement This role forms part of Museum and Cultural Institutions team within the Community and Regional Engagement Function and is aligned to the International and External Engagement Portfolio within the University. Accounts Receivable Officer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 Finance The role supports effective financial administration through consistent payment receipting and compliant receivables processing and adherence to university policies and procedures. Senior Business Analyst Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 People Advisory and Wellbeing Lead process improvement and transformation to strengthen pay accuracy, compliance, controls and sustainable business practices across Adelaide University. Hear about jobs that are right for you! Sign up to job alerts First Name (required) c9190850 Last Name (required) b0e7e28d Email (required) 3b544147 Job Alert 5123c595 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Casual Campaign Casual Campaign Locations Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia Portfolio College of Creative Arts, Design and Humanities College of Creative Arts, Design and Humanities Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Adelaide University respectfully acknowledges the Kaurna, Boandik and Barngarla First Nations Peoples and their Elders past and present, who are the First Nations’ Traditional Owners of the lands that will be home to our campuses located in Adelaide, Magill, Mawson Lakes, Waite, Roseworthy, Mount Gambier and Whyalla. Disclaimer | Copyright | Privacy | Accessibility | Cookie Preferences Facebook Instagram LinkedIn Tiktok YouTube Weixin Australian University Provider Number PRV14404 | CRICOS Provider Number 04249J Candidate Details Please choose a portfolio"
    },
    "https://jobs.adelaide.edu.au/jobs/emergency-and-critical-care-veterinarian-casual-opportunities-roseworthy-sa-australia": {
      "title": "Emergency and Critical Care Veterinarian",
      "description": "Emergency and Critical Care Veterinarian - Casual Opportunities - Roseworthy, SA, Australia Manage your settings Search Jobs Visit adelaideuni.edu.au Kiosk mode Together, we will define Adelaide University Together, we will define Adelaide University Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Jobs near me Emergency and Critical Care Veterinarian - Casual Opportunities Roseworthy, SA, Australia, 5371 HEO7 Casual HEO8 Add to favourites View favourites Based at Roseworthy Campus Casual role, various shifts available on a 24/7 roster (HEO 8) $76.09 to $85.36 per hour plus an employer contribution of 12% superannuation applies. In addition, loadings between 15% and 40% apply when working outside the normal span of hours At Adelaide University, we create the opportunities you need to achieve your ambitions – because when you thrive, we thrive. We are transforming education for contemporary learners and global citizens. Building on a proud legacy and shaped by bold ambition, it’s a place of excellence and equity, where our vibrant community of staff are united by our purpose to inspire Australia’s future change-makers and create a better tomorrow. Work that matters As a member of the Emergency and Critical Care (ECC) team at the Roseworthy Veterinary Hospital, you will provide high-quality care to a varied and growing caseload of small animal patients through a 24/7 roster. You will have access to advanced imaging modalities, including CT, and work closely with a dedicated radiographer and onsite veterinary diagnostic laboratory, enabling collaboration with pathologists to achieve the best patient outcomes. As part of the Roseworthy Veterinary Hospital team, you will provide high-quality emergency and critical care for small animals while contributing to the clinical education of future veterinarians. Your work will directly impact patient outcomes, student learning, and the ongoing development of a vibrant teaching hospital. Curious to learn more? Explore the full role description to discover more about this opportunity. The team You will join a motivated and experienced team of veterinarians, nurses, and support staff delivering emergency and critical care services 24/7. Reporting to the Head of Emergency and Critical Care, your role will contribute to both patient care and veterinary education. Supported by a team of experienced academic specialists, you will engage in daily case discussions and contribute to complex clinical decision-making. In addition, you will play a key role in the education of final-year veterinary students, involving them in case management and providing hands-on learning opportunities while modeling the highest standards of clinical and professional practice, helping to shape the next generation of veterinarians. Visit the AU website to learn more about the Roseworthy Veterinary Hospital Our people Our people are guided by purpose, curiosity, and a commitment to lifelong learning. We embrace authenticity, innovation, and collaboration, and harness diverse thinking in our pursuit of excellence. This role is perfect for someone who enjoys a dynamic and varied clinical environment, working with a wide range of patients, colleagues, and final-year students, keeping each workday engaging and rewarding. Learn more about our people, what we stand for, and what we offer at Careers at AU. Experience To join our community and thrive in this role, you will likely have the following skills and experience: Veterinary qualification with eligibility to be registered in South Australia Experience in first-opinion or emergency services, including emergency surgery Strong communication and interpersonal skills with clients and team members Enthusiasm and experience in assisting, teaching, or mentoring others High-level written communication skills, including medical records, referral letters, and client correspondence Our commitment to inclusion and diversity We are committed to fostering a culture of inclusion where diversity is celebrated and everyone feels respected and valued. Adelaide University is an equal opportunity employer, supporting flexible working arrangements and reasonable adjustments throughout the recruitment process. Launch your future with Adelaide University now Applying is simple. Simply click on the Apply Now button and upload: Your current CV A cover letter that tells us why you’re excited about the role This will remain open until all roles filled The University reserves the right to close this advertisement before the closing date if a suitable candidate is identified. For further information about this opportunity, please contact: Simone Patterson Talent Acquisition Adviser +61 8 8302 1700 | careers.adelaideuniversity@adelaide.edu.au #LI-DNI #S-DNI Ready to start your application? Apply here. First Name (required) 46f043ea Last Name (required) dbde8860 Email (required) f1103702 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Ready to start your application? Apply here. First Name (required) 32e50b54 Last Name (required) 67850b18 Email (required) 55e0edd4 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Geoff Williams Fellowship in Physics N/A, SA, Australia School of Physics, Chemistry and Earth Sciences Build your academic career through physics research, teaching, mentoring and collaboration across leading research programs. Exhibitions Coordinator Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 Community and Regional Engagement This role forms part of Museum and Cultural Institutions team within the Community and Regional Engagement Function and is aligned to the International and External Engagement Portfolio within the University. Accounts Receivable Officer Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 Finance The role supports effective financial administration through consistent payment receipting and compliant receivables processing and adherence to university policies and procedures. Senior Business Analyst Adelaide CBD, SA, Australia, 5000 People Advisory and Wellbeing Lead process improvement and transformation to strengthen pay accuracy, compliance, controls and sustainable business practices across Adelaide University. Hear about jobs that are right for you! Sign up to job alerts First Name (required) e4ef1454 Last Name (required) de5911d2 Email (required) 079aff91 Job Alert a845de52 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories HEO7 HEO7 Locations Roseworthy, SA, Australia Roseworthy, SA, Australia Portfolio College of Science College of Science Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Adelaide University respectfully acknowledges the Kaurna, Boandik and Barngarla First Nations Peoples and their Elders past and present, who are the First Nations’ Traditional Owners of the lands that will be home to our campuses located in Adelaide, Magill, Mawson Lakes, Waite, Roseworthy, Mount Gambier and Whyalla. Disclaimer | Copyright | Privacy | Accessibility | Cookie Preferences Facebook Instagram LinkedIn Tiktok YouTube Weixin Australian University Provider Number PRV14404 | CRICOS Provider Number 04249J Candidate Details Please choose a portfolio"
